<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paulicians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Paulicianism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Paulicians were a Christian sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, recorded from the 7th century, who rejected the church hierarchy and the veneration of icons and were described by their opponents as dualists. Persecuted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, they set up a militarised state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> in the 9th century and raided imperial lands until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> destroyed it in the 870s. Many were resettled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, where their ideas influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogomilism">Bogomils</a>.</p></div>
